@startuml PassTheKeysDefended
title Defended - passkey-bound sessions: the binding key lives in the authenticator

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 14
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Victim" as V
box "The victim's device" #E7F6EC
  participant "Authenticator\n(passkey, holds the private key)" as AUTH
  participant "Victim's tab (/login)\n+ PasskeyProvider" as VP
end box
participant "Relying party\nverifies + binds the session" as RP
box "Attacker's site (separate origin)" #FDECEC
  participant "Attacker's tab" as AP
  participant "Back-end\ndead-drop + proxy" as AD
end box
actor "Attacker" as A

note across
  There is NO key generated in the page. The sender-constraining key is the passkey credential
  itself: its private key lives in the authenticator and never leaves it. The session is bound
  to the public key of whichever credential signs the login, and each request is authorized by
  a fresh WebAuthn proof-of-possession assertion. The page can neither choose nor read the key.
end note

== Attacker starts a login, but will not finish it ==
A -> AP : open the attacker site
AP -> RP : start a passkey login (via proxy)
RP --> AP : nonce **Ca**
AP -> AD : publish **Ca** in the dead-drop

== Victim's page is compromised and relays the sign-in ==
V -> VP : click "Sign in"
VP -> AD : fetch the nonce (cross-origin) -> **Ca**
VP -> AUTH : get(**Ca**)  (user verifies)
AUTH --> VP : assertion over **Ca**, signed by the passkey\n(the private key stays in the authenticator)
VP -> AD : hand over the assertion\n(there is no page-held key to leak)

== Attacker finishes the login - and is stuck ==
AP -> AD : take the assertion
AP -> RP : finish the login with it
RP -> RP : verify the signature; bind the session\ntoken to the credential's public key (jkt)
RP --> AP : a real session token for the victim  (bound to the passkey)
AP -> RP : GET /api/me  (Authorization: Passkey <token>)
note right of AP : a request needs a FRESH assertion from the\nvictim's authenticator; this machine has none
RP --> AP : **401** - missing passkey proof
AP -> A : "Session captured, but locked"

note across #E7F6EC
  The binding key is in the authenticator, below the page. Finishing the ceremony is not enough:
  every resource call needs a fresh proof signed by the passkey, the attacker's machine has no
  authenticator, and the key never leaves the victim's device. A compromised page can coax at
  most one more user-approved proof out of the authenticator - it can never hand over the key,
  so there is no durable, relayable session.
end note

@enduml
